WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.227
You tap your card at a supermarket in Baku. The payment

00:03.327 --> 00:07.098
has to pass through four systems: the terminal, the shop's bank,

00:07.198 --> 00:10.909
the card network, and your own bank. Each one works ninety-nine

00:11.009 --> 00:14.842
percent of the time. What's the chance your payment goes through?

00:14.933 --> 00:18.351
The trick of this lecture is to write the event you care about in

00:18.451 --> 00:22.356
terms of simpler events, and then let the laws of probability do the work.

00:23.950 --> 00:27.407
Success means every link works: the terminal and the acquirer

00:27.507 --> 00:30.615
and the network and the issuer. That's an intersection.

00:30.700 --> 00:34.272
If the links fail independently, multiply: point nine nine

00:34.372 --> 00:37.881
to the fourth, about ninety-six percent. Every extra link

00:37.981 --> 00:40.160
in a chain shaves a little more off.

00:41.250 --> 00:44.479
Look at what happens as the chain grows. With fifty links,

00:44.579 --> 00:48.669
each ninety-nine percent reliable, the whole chain works only about sixty

00:48.769 --> 00:51.941
percent of the time. In a series system, the weakest link

00:52.041 --> 00:54.524
isn't the only problem: every link costs you.

00:55.616 --> 00:59.631
Now flip it around. A data centre has two independent power feeds,

00:59.731 --> 01:02.562
and it stays up as long as at least one of them

01:02.662 --> 01:05.991
works. Each feed is up ninety-five percent of the time.

01:06.083 --> 01:09.626
Now success is a union: feed one or feed two. The easy

01:09.726 --> 01:13.607
way in is the complement. The centre goes down only if both

01:13.707 --> 01:18.195
feeds fail: five percent times five percent, a quarter of a percent.

01:18.295 --> 01:22.985
So it's up ninety-nine point seven five percent of the time. Redundancy

01:23.085 --> 01:26.358
turns two mediocre parts into an excellent system.

01:27.450 --> 01:30.745
Real systems mix the two. Here are three stages in a

01:30.845 --> 01:34.207
row, and each stage has a backup. Break it down: each

01:34.307 --> 01:37.798
stage is a parallel pair, and the stages form a series.

01:37.883 --> 01:41.124
If each unit works ninety percent of the time, a stage

01:41.224 --> 01:44.465
fails only when both units do: one in a hundred. Three

01:44.565 --> 01:48.672
stages in series then work point nine nine cubed: about ninety-seven

01:48.772 --> 01:53.436
percent. From ninety-percent parts! Composition is just this: name the simple

01:53.536 --> 01:56.591
events, glue them with 'and' and 'or', and compute.

01:57.683 --> 02:00.644
So: in series, multiply the chances of success.

02:00.744 --> 02:03.835
In parallel, multiply the chances of failure, and

02:03.935 --> 02:07.222
take the complement. Everything else is bookkeeping.
